(1783289, 236008, 31403, '<td valign="top">this is a terrific shot.  it pains me to read the close minded comments made on this photo - please don''t let the amatuer comments on this discourage you.  What do they know?<br/> <br/>Go remove that phone pole, and go make some money with this, or print and frame it and give it to a friend - the world is a little better for you having taken this, go share it</td>', 'this is a terrific shot.  it pains me to read the close minded comments made on this photo - please don''t let the amatuer comments on this discourage you.  What do they know? Go remove that phone pole, and go make some money with this, or print and frame it and give it to a friend - the world is a little better for you having taken this, go share it', '2005-10-14 01:28:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(1783542, 236995, 38509, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by pasaro:</b></div><hr/><i> I can''t understand such a low score for this. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>LOL, waht to say ? neither do i :-)<br/> <br/>thanks</td>', 'Originally posted by pasaro: I can''t understand such a low score for this.   LOL, waht to say ? neither do i :-) thanks', '2005-10-14 08:04:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(1785238, 237119, 19831, '<td valign="top">A cool point of view on this shot. I like the simple, clean, yet firm composition. Exposure looks good. It looks like the cross is not quite straight. It could strengthen this presentation if it were perfectly straight up, a little bit bolder. (you may already know this) but you can straighten shots easily in editing. In ps, you can choose rotate, then arbitrary rotation and put in your degrees. You can even use the ruler tool first, draw a line along a straight edge in the photo, then pick arbitrary rotation and it will automatically figure the degrees to straighten perfectly. Most editing software should have something similar. This shot might also be cool to come back and shoot the same thing sometime with a really dramatic sky.</td>', 'A cool point of view on this shot. I like the simple, clean, yet firm composition. Exposure looks good. It looks like the cross is not quite straight. It could strengthen this presentation if it were perfectly straight up, a little bit bolder. (you may already know this) but you can straighten shots easily in editing. In ps, you can choose rotate, then arbitrary rotation and put in your degrees. You can even use the ruler tool first, draw a line along a straight edge in the photo, then pick arbitrary rotation and it will automatically figure the degrees to straighten perfectly. Most editing software should have something similar. This shot might also be cool to come back and shoot the same thing sometime with a really dramatic sky.', '2005-10-15 00:58:43.000000', NULL, 0),
